Q1: Why do these commands behave differently?

ls ; date
ls ";" date

The semicolon (;) is a command separator that allows multiple commands to be executed sequentially.  
So:

ls ; date  
runs ls first, then runs date.

ls ";" date  
treats ";" and "date" as filenames passed to ls, so ls tries to list files named ; and date instead of running the date command.

